                        1 "IAL"
                        2 ************************************************************************
                        3 *
                        4 *  INVERSE ASSEMBLER FOR THE DEC PDP-11 (UNIBUS)
                        5 *
                        6 *  For HP 1650/1660/1670, 16500 and 16600/16700 series logic analyzers.
                        7 *  Assemble with the HP 10391B Inverse Assembler Development Package:
                        8 *
                        9 *      ASM IPDP11.S
                       10 *
                       11 *  Download / convert with IALDOWN (or the 16700 IA Format utility)
                       12 *  using "Invasm" field option B (Invasm field, no pop-up).
                       13 *
                       14 ************************************************************************
                       15 *
                       16 *  ANALYZER LABELS (all Unibus signals are asserted low; set the label
                       17 *  polarity to negative "-" in the Format menu so that 1 = asserted):
                       18 *
                       19 *    ADDR   18 bits   A<17:00>
                       20 *    DATA   16 bits   D<15:00>
                       21 *    STAT    3 bits   bit 0 = C0
                       22 *                     bit 1 = C1
                       23 *                     bit 2 = INTR  (connect to ground if not probed)
                       24 *
                       25 *    C1 C0   0 0  DATI    0 1  DATIP    1 0  DATO    1 1  DATOB
                       26 *
                       27 *  STATE CLOCK: the negating (trailing) edge of MSYN.  Address, data
                       28 *  and C1/C0 are all still valid on the bus at that point for every
                       29 *  CPU and DMA transfer.
                       30 *
                       31 ************************************************************************
                       32 *
                       33 *  HOW IT WORKS
                       34 *
                       35 *  The Unibus does not say whether a DATI is an instruction fetch or a
                       36 *  data read, so the inverse assembler must be synchronized by hand:
                       37 *  roll a state that is an instruction fetch to the top of the listing
                       38 *  and select the "Invasm" field.  From then on, each decoded
                       39 *  instruction looks ahead in the trace:
                       40 *
                       41 *  - the extension words (immediate, absolute, index) are the reads of
                       42 *    the following addresses; they are tagged "operand",
                       43 *  - the next instruction fetch is predicted from the instruction:
                       44 *    the next sequential address, either branch destination, a jump
                       45 *    target taken from an extension word, or (for RTS PC, RTI, RTT and
                       46 *    indirect JMP/JSR) the data of the read that fetched the new PC,
                       47 *  - a read from the vector area (below VEC_LIMIT) followed by a read
                       48 *    of the next word is taken to be an interrupt or trap vector; the
                       49 *    new PC is the data of the first of those two reads,
                       50 *  - every other cycle in between is tagged as a data cycle.
                       51 *
                       52 *  The predicted next fetch is tagged and is decoded when the listing
                       53 *  reaches it, so the chain continues for as long as the analyzer
                       54 *  keeps finding the next fetch within SCANMAX states.
                       55 *
                       56 ************************************************************************
HEWLETT-PACKARD: Inverse Assembly Language                           Sun Oct 11 15:03:54 2026                              PAGE    2
